✅ Theory for Bank Customer Churn Prediction Using Neural Networks
Objective

The goal is to build an Artificial Neural Network (ANN) model that predicts whether a bank customer will leave the bank (churn) within the next 6 months based on their demographic and financial profile.

The Kaggle dataset contains 10,000 customers and 14 features, including:

CreditScore

Geography

Gender

Age

Tenure

Balance

Number of Products

HasCrCard

IsActiveMember

EstimatedSalary

The target variable is:
✅ Exited – 1 (Customer left)
✅ Exited – 0 (Customer stayed)

✅ 1. Reading the Dataset

The dataset is loaded using pandas (e.g., read_csv).
Key steps involve:

Understanding data types

Checking for missing values

Inspecting target variable distribution

Exploring basic statistics

The dataset is already clean and contains no missing values.

✅ 2. Feature–Target Separation and Train-Test Split
Feature Set (X)

All independent variables except “Exited”.

Target Set (y)

Exited (0 or 1)

Encoding Categorical Variables

Neural networks require numerical inputs.
Therefore:

Label Encoding for Gender

One-Hot Encoding for Geography

Splitting the Data

Use an 80-20 or 70-30 split:

Training Set → used to learn model weights

Test Set → used to evaluate performance

✅ 3. Normalizing Train and Test Data

ANNs perform best when input features are on similar scales.

We use StandardScaler or MinMaxScaler, which transforms features so:

mean = 0

variance = 1
(or range 0–1 for MinMax)

Normalization helps the model:

converge faster

avoid exploding gradients

perform better on numerical stability

✅ 4. Initializing and Building the Neural Network Model

A typical ANN architecture used here:

✅ Input Layer

Number of neurons = number of input features (e.g., 11 after encoding)

✅ Hidden Layers

1 or 2 Dense layers with:

ReLU activation

6–16 neurons

Regularization if needed: dropout, L2, etc.

✅ Output Layer

1 neuron

Activation = Sigmoid

Output = probability of customer churn (0–1)

✅ Model Compilation

Optimizer: Adam

Loss Function: Binary Crossentropy

Metric: Accuracy

✅ Training the Model

Run for 50–100 epochs depending on performance.

Common Issues and Improvements

✅ Adding more hidden layers
✅ Using Dropout to reduce overfitting
✅ Increasing training epochs
✅ Adjusting learning rate
✅ Hyperparameter tuning (batch size, optimizer, activation)

Improvement involves experimentation to achieve higher accuracy and better generalization.

✅ 5. Evaluation: Accuracy Score & Confusion Matrix

After predictions:

✅ Accuracy

Percentage of correct predictions.

✅ Confusion Matrix

Shows:

TP – True Positives (correct churn predictions)

TN – True Negatives (correct non-churn predictions)

FP – False Positives

FN – False Negatives

The confusion matrix helps us understand:

how many churners were correctly identified

whether the model is biased toward predicting non-churn

✅ Typical Performance

A well-designed ANN can achieve:

80–87% accuracy

Good recall and precision for the churn class

✅ Short Viva Questions & Answers
Q1: Why do we normalize the data?

To ensure all features contribute equally and help the ANN train efficiently.

Q2: Why use Sigmoid in the output layer?

Because it outputs a value between 0 and 1 for binary classification.

Q3: What is Binary Crossentropy?

A loss function that measures how well probabilities match binary labels.

Q4: Why do we need One-Hot Encoding?

To convert categorical variables (like Geography) into numeric form.

Q5: Why does ANN perform well for churn prediction?

Because it can capture complex relationships between multiple features.
